.venv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# Audit output. An audit writes into whatever directory it is run from, and this
# repository is the obvious place to run one, so every file a run can produce has to
# be listed here or it ends up in a commit. Two of them did.
#
# `checklist-results-crawl.json` is the crawl inventory, and it is derived from the
# `--json` path rather than named separately, so adding the inventory in 0.9.0 added
# an output nobody thought to ignore. The `-crawl` glob is deliberately wider than
# the one filename for that reason: the next derived artifact should already be
# covered. There is a test that checks this list against the runner's own defaults.
checklist-results.json
checklist-results*-crawl.json
CHECKLIST-REPORT.md
CHECKLIST.html
LLM-QUEUE*.md
.seo-runs/

# `tools/probe_shapes.py` output. The probe is run from this directory too, and it
# writes twice: the skeleton it exists to produce, and the crawl inventory it takes
# first so the site-wide items have an input. Only the skeleton was listed here, and
# the inventory was committed twice over — a crawl of the local fixture server in
# 0.9.0, which then sat in `scripts/` unread for fifteen releases, and a crawl of a
# client's live site in 0.25.0, every URL, title and meta description of it. Same
# drift as the audit outputs above, one directory over, which is also why the test
# that guards those said nothing: it compared filenames against paths, so it could
# only see the top of the checkout. It now reads these literals out of the tool and
# compares by basename.
probe-raw.json
probe-inventory.json

# The live-path check in .github/workflows/ci.yml, run by hand. Reproducing that
# step locally is the only way to know whether it will pass, so it is worth doing
# and its output is worth ignoring.
blocked.json
blocked.log
live.json
live.md
live.html
live.log
live-crawl.json
live-queue*.md
orphans.json
report.log
server.log

.DS_Store
.env
